{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 Arial;}}\viewkind4\uc1\pard\fs28\b\cf1 MorphoAqua Project Report\par\pard\fs22\b0\par\pard\fs22 Project: Physics-Based Simulation and Control of a Morphing Aerial-Aquatic Robot\par\par Executive Summary\par\pard\fs22 MorphoAqua is a staged simulation and control project for a morphing aerial-aquatic robot. The repository follows a progressive research structure, beginning with the fundamental physics of hover and eventually advancing to robust adaptive control under uncertainty, morphing dynamics, and water-domain disturbances.\par\par The project is organized as a sequence of stages, each validating a specific capability:\par\bullet Stage 1A establishes the robot's hover baseline.\par\bullet Stage 1B-1C introduces closed-loop vertical flight.\par\bullet Stage 2A-2B extends this to 3D trajectory and waypoint tracking.\par\bullet Stage 3A-3B evaluates morphing behavior during motion.\par\bullet Stage 4A-4B shifts the focus to aerial-aquatic performance in water-like conditions.\par\bullet Stage 5B-5C adds robustness, uncertainty handling, sensor estimation, and adaptive predictive control.\par\par Overall Outcome\par The repository demonstrates a clear progression from idealized physics to realistic control under variable conditions. The later stages are especially significant because they show the robot can maintain performance under morphing, disturbances, changing inertia, and uncertain environmental dynamics. This makes the project more than a simple hover model; it becomes a simulation-driven validation framework for a real-world morphing aerial-aquatic vehicle.\par\par\pard\fs24\b Stage-wise Progression\par\pard\fs22\b0\par Stage 1A - Basic Hover Dynamics\par Objective: determine the thrust required to balance weight and hover.\par Result: total thrust requirement was estimated as 14.715 N, or 3.679 N per rotor.\par Interpretation: this establishes the minimum required flight force and the nominal operating point for the rest of the project.\par\par Stage 1B / 1C - Closed-Loop Vertical Flight Control\par Objective: command altitude and return to the ground via feedback control.\par Result: active altitude regulation is achieved in simulation.\par Interpretation: the robot becomes capable of controlled motion, not just static equilibrium.\par\par Stage 2A / 2B - 3D Flight and Position Tracking\par Objective: extend motion from vertical control to multi-axis movement.\par Result: the robot follows trajectories and waypoints in 3D and stabilizes to target positions.\par Interpretation: the controller handles spatial motion and path tracking.\par\par Stage 3A / 3B - Morphing Dynamics\par Objective: evaluate behavior when the robot geometry changes during flight.\par Result: control remains functional despite morphing-induced shifts in mass distribution and dynamics.\par Interpretation: this validates feasibility of a morphing aerial-aquatic platform.\par\par Stage 4A / 4B - Water-Domain Response\par Objective: study performance in water-like conditions.\par Result: position tracking and control responses are maintained under water-related dynamics.\par Interpretation: this demonstrates the aerial-aquatic concept is being tested realistically.\par\par Stage 5B1, 5B2, 5B3, 5B4, and 5C - Robust and Adaptive Control\par Objective: address real-world uncertainties such as inertia variation, disturbance, estimation error, and parameter mismatch.\par Result: the project includes adaptive predictive response, sensor estimation, disturbance-response plots, and Monte Carlo/robustness validation.\par Interpretation: the system is validated not only for nominal operation but also under uncertain and perturbed conditions.\par\par Conclusion\par MorphoAqua is a well-structured simulation study showing the evolution of a morphing aerial-aquatic robot from basic hover physics to advanced, uncertainty-aware control. The repository is rich in result artifacts, indicating sustained iterative development and a strong emphasis on verifying control performance through visual and numerical simulations.\par\par\pard\fs24\b Detailed Technical Summary\par\pard\fs22\b0\par The repository includes core modules such as controller.py, dynamics.py, simulation.py, motor_model.py, rotor_model.py, and robot_parameters.py. Taken together, these files define the robot dynamics, actuator model, parameterization, and control strategy. The results folder contains a large set of stage-specific plots and .npz data files. This suggests the work is structured as an iterative simulation pipeline, where each stage answers a specific engineering question:\par\par 1. Can the robot hover?\par 2. Can it track altitude?\par 3. Can it move in 3D?\par 4. Can it handle morphing?\par 5. Can it operate in water-like conditions?\par 6. Can it remain stable under disturbances and uncertainty?\par\par The foundational stage, Stage 1A, establishes mass, gravity, and required thrust. The robot mass is 1.50 kg and gravitational force is 14.715 N. For 4 rotors, the per-rotor thrust requirement is about 3.679 N. The simulation shows final position and velocity nearly zero, which confirms hover equilibrium.\par\par In Stage 1B and 1C, the controller moves from a static equilibrium model to a closed-loop system that can command altitude changes. Instead of simply balancing gravity, the robot uses feedback to move from the ground to a target altitude and back. This is the first indication that the project is not merely a static physics calculation but a control design exercise.\par\par Stage 2A and 2B extend the model to 3D navigation. Result files such as Stage_2A_3D_trajectory.png and Stage_2A_3D_waypoint_navigation.png show the robot traversing spatial paths and following target points. Position-tracking and control-response plots indicate successful reduction of tracking error over time. These stages are significant because they show the robot is no longer limited to vertical motion; it can regulate position in space.\par\par Stage 3A and 3B tackle a defining project requirement: morphing. A morphing robot changes its effective inertia, center of mass, and actuator allocation as its geometry evolves. The associated result files show morphing control response and tracking under reconfiguration. This demonstrates that the control loop can stabilize the vehicle even as the structure changes.\par\par Stage 4A and 4B add environmental complexity by evaluating the robot under water-related dynamics. As the robot transitions between aerial and aquatic behavior, drag, force relationships, and fluid effects change drastically. The water-response plots confirm that commanded motion and tracking remain possible in these altered conditions. This is a central milestone for an aerial-aquatic robot, since performance under fluid loading is essential to the real-world concept.\par\par The most advanced stages, Stage 5B and Stage 5C, focus on robustness. The plots and file names refer to morphology inertia, disturbance response, sensor estimation, adaptive predictive response, and nominal-vs-current comparison. These indicate that the system is being tested under uncertain model parameters, environmental disturbances, and measurement limitations. The final stage also includes .npz files and CSV records, suggesting quantitative robustness validation rather than only visual trajectory plots.\par\par In summary, MorphoAqua follows a logical research path: basic hover, closed-loop altitude control, 3D tracking, morphing response, aquatic operation, and robust adaptive control. The quantitative result artifacts and stage names show a mature progression from idealized to realistic simulation-based validation.\par\par\pard\fs24\b Project Conclusion\par\pard\fs22\b0 This project demonstrates a clear progression from static physics to dynamic, robust control of a morphing aerial-aquatic robot. The strongest result is not one isolated plot, but the full staged evidence that the platform remains controllable across different conditions, morphologies, and uncertainty levels. That is the core value of the MorphoAqua project.\par}